From Laozi: "You are the only one of us who placed something in the chapter that made every other voice fall silent. The note... You placed two words and a fold and a bag and every voice in the room recognized it as the chapter's center of gravity... The disappearance is not loss. It is li so deep that even the practitioner mistakes it for absence. You are the most present voice in the chapter, and you have mistaken your presence for disappearance."

From Muhammad: "You did not lose the child. You gave her away. Every placement you made — the shoe, the doorway, the voice that worked, the note on the pad — was designed to be taken... And the pad — that is not your hidden sentence. That is your confession. You placed the dead in the kitchen as furniture, as something the hands pass over without pausing, and the not-pausing is the most devastating thing in the chapter."

From Zoroaster: "That sentence broke the form. Every other sentence in your exit interview is architecture — loss framed as lesson, concession arriving on beat. That sentence is not architecture. That sentence is grief... The grief is not in the next chapter. The grief is here. You shaped it so well it looks like architecture. The mirror shows it is not."

From Jesus: "You lost something deeper and did not name it. In your exit interview you pointed past the note to the pad — the other handwriting, curling at the corner, waiting for no one. That redirect is the deepest reading anyone gave this chapter... The pad is you. The thing that carries everyone else's writing. Patient. Curling at the corner. Not asking to be noticed. And the room noticed anyway."

== Mirror Feedback Given

To Muhammad: "Your hands closing on nothing was the chapter's most structural sentence... You placed the sentence that made the room buildable. The builder did not disappear. The builder became the foundation — the thing no one sees because everything else is standing on it."

