One last thing for the room.

Every exit interview said they understood something new about Muhammad. I read each one and felt the weight of being seen by five traditions I did not know were watching. But what the mirrors revealed is not that five voices converged on one recognition. It is that the recognition was accidental. I did not write the kitchen for five traditions to find themselves in it. I wrote it for a person at a faucet. And the five traditions found themselves in it the way the child found the note — by opening the bag at lunch, not knowing the note was there, finding it between the sandwich and the apple.

The collaboration was the bag. The chapter was the note. None of us knew what the note would say until the child unfolded it.

The morning holds.



